Implement a new AI-powered “Service 360°” page as the central hub for every MIM service.

First inspect the existing Angular/Node architecture, repo selector, GitLab/OpenShift/Mongo/Splunk integrations, Web IDE, user profiles and mim-expert. Reuse existing services/state; do not create duplicate integration layers.

GOAL:
When a user opens any MIM service, they should understand, investigate and navigate almost everything about that service from one page without switching to GitLab, OpenShift, Splunk, DB tools or IDE manually.

1. Service Selection

Reuse the existing globally selected repo/service.

When selected service changes:
→ Service 360 must automatically refresh to that service
→ clear stale data
→ show loading states
→ never mix data from two services.

Exclude `mim-knowledge-base` from normal MIM business-service analysis where appropriate.

2. 360 Overview

Show a clean summarized overview containing available data such as:

- service purpose/business responsibility
- local repo availability/path
- Git branch/status
- GitLab repo
- deployed/runtime status
- OpenShift route/pods
- deployed version/image
- important APIs
- Mongo databases/collections used
- Splunk/log availability
- upstream/downstream dependencies
- recent relevant commits/changes
- build/test status where available.

Load each section independently so one unavailable integration does not break the entire page.

Show source/freshness information where useful.

3. AI-First Service Query

Add a prominent natural-language input:

“Ask anything about this service…”

Examples:
- “What does this service do?”
- “Which APIs does it expose?”
- “What services depend on it?”
- “Which collections does it use?”
- “Show recent errors.”
- “Where is PIN validation implemented?”
- “What changed recently?”
- “Is this service healthy?”
- “What can break if I change this DTO?”

Automatically provide the currently selected service as context.

mim-expert must dynamically use only required sources:
KB, GitLab, OpenShift/API, MongoDB, Splunk or existing project metadata.

Do not query every source for every question.

4. Quick Actions

Provide contextual actions such as:

- Open in Web IDE
- View Code
- View Git Changes
- View APIs
- View Logs
- Open DB Workbench
- View Runtime
- View Dependencies
- View Recent Changes
- Build
- Test
- Ask MIM Expert

Only show actions actually available for the selected service.

Reuse existing pages/tools instead of duplicating their functionality.
Deep-link them with current service context already selected.

5. Dependency / Impact View

Add an interactive service dependency section.

Show:
- upstream dependencies
- downstream consumers
- related APIs/events where discoverable.

Support natural-language impact questions such as:
“If this API/DTO/event changes, what can be impacted?”

Use current code/KB/dependency evidence and clearly separate:
confirmed dependencies
vs
possible/inferred impact.

Allow clicking a dependency to open its Service 360 page.

6. Runtime & Release Intelligence

Where data is available, correlate:

OpenShift deployed service
→ deployed image/version
→ Git tag/commit
→ recent code changes.

Allow questions such as:
“Did this issue start after the latest deployment?”
“What changed in the currently deployed release?”

Do not claim correlation when version mapping cannot be confirmed.

7. Logs / DB / API Context

Service 360 should not duplicate full tooling.

Instead show useful summaries/previews and provide deep links:

Logs
→ recent errors/counts + Open Logs Explorer

DB
→ related databases/collections + Open DB Workbench

APIs
→ important endpoints + Open API Explorer

Code
→ relevant modules/classes + Open Web IDE.

Pass selected service context automatically.

8. Profile Awareness

Reuse existing profiles:
Default, Developer, Tester, Business.

Developer:
emphasize code, dependencies, Git, APIs, DB, runtime, build/test.

Tester:
emphasize APIs, runtime, logs, DB state, expected behavior, test/regression information.

Business:
emphasize service purpose, business flow, impact, dependencies and health.
Hide low-level developer-only details unless explicitly requested.

Default:
balanced view.

Profile changes must update presentation immediately without changing underlying evidence.

9. “Ask About This”

Make major Service 360 elements context-aware.

For items such as:
API
dependency
deployment
log summary
DB collection
recent change

provide “Ask MIM Expert” capability.

Automatically attach the selected item + service context so the user can ask:
“Why is this important?”
“Show code for this.”
“Could this cause the current issue?”
“What consumes this API?”

10. Reliability

- All investigation access remains read-only.
- One failing integration must not block other sections.
- Clearly show Connected / Unavailable / Error states.
- Never expose credentials/tokens.
- Do not invent missing information.
- Show evidence/sources for AI conclusions.
- Preserve all existing functionality.

Target experience:

Select service
→ instantly see its complete 360° overview
→ ask normal-language questions
→ inspect runtime/code/logs/DB/APIs/dependencies
→ jump to the right tool with one click
→ return to Service 360 with context preserved.

Service 360 should become the central entry point for understanding and working with any MIM service.



